  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This literature review synthesizes 95 studies that utilize smartphone application GPS data to analyze pedestrian mobility. It categorizes these data sources into three distinct types—fitness/activity trackers, application aggregators, and travel-survey apps—and details how their specific collection, sharing, and processing methods shape their utility for pedestrian research.",
      "why": "It establishes a systematic framework for understanding the trade-offs of different app-based data sources, allowing researchers and planners to match specific pedestrian planning questions—such as assessing spatial inequalities or walking environments—with the most appropriate GPS data type."
    }
